1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:01,502
London, 1980.

2
00:00:01,733 --> 00:00:05,789
Six gunmen, twenty six hostages,
and a country about to watch it live.

3
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:13,100
The explosion ripped through the building.

4
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:19,556
This is the story of those six days, and of what
the secret Home Office history says about them.

5
00:00:30,247 --> 00:00:32,839
On the morning of 30 April 1980,

6
00:00:33,029 --> 00:00:39,143
six armed men burst into the Iranian embassy
at 16 Princes Gate, in South Kensington, London.

7
00:00:39,534 --> 00:00:43,327
They took twenty six people hostage,
among them embassy staff,

8
00:00:43,537 --> 00:00:46,910
visitors, and a British police
constable who guarded the door.

9
00:00:47,661 --> 00:00:51,200
The gunmen were young Arab Iranians
from the province of Khuzestan,

10
00:00:51,471 --> 00:00:54,549
and they opposed the new government
of the Ayatollah Khomeini.

11
00:00:54,981 --> 00:00:58,401
They demanded the release
of ninety one prisoners held in Iran,

12
00:00:58,672 --> 00:01:00,808
and a safe way out of Britain for themselves.

13
00:01:01,501 --> 00:01:06,589
It began only days after an American attempt
to rescue the hostages held in Tehran had failed.

14
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:09,476
Two of the hostages worked for the BBC.

15
00:01:10,168 --> 00:01:14,829
Chris Cramer and Sim Harris had come
to the embassy to ask for visas to Iran.

16
00:01:15,481 --> 00:01:17,615
Here is Sim Harris on the first hours.

17
00:01:18,277 --> 00:01:23,767
I didn't feel my life was in danger
in those early parts of the siege.

18
00:01:25,267 --> 00:01:30,067
I always thought it was a bit
more of a stunt than anything else.

19
00:01:30,347 --> 00:01:34,481
Outside, Scotland Yard's anti-terrorist
branch sealed off the streets

20
00:01:34,531 --> 00:01:36,763
and sent negotiators to the embassy door.

21
00:01:37,483 --> 00:01:39,563
You need to know exactly where everybody is,

22
00:01:39,583 --> 00:01:46,633
and whether they are hostages or the bad guys. So
there were people watching all the windows all the
time.

23
00:01:47,475 --> 00:01:50,391
The gunmen told a reporter
that if their demands failed,

24
00:01:50,562 --> 00:01:54,682
they would kill the hostages and blow
up the embassy after twenty four hours.

25
00:01:55,614 --> 00:02:01,476
Among the hostages was PC Trevor Lock, who carried
a revolver and kept it hidden from the gunmen.

26
00:02:02,127 --> 00:02:06,763
In Hereford, the Army's Special Air Service,
the SAS, were already on standby.

27
00:02:12,254 --> 00:02:17,789
The gunmen's leader was called Salim, and the police
gave him a field telephone to talk to the negotiators.

28
00:02:18,661 --> 00:02:21,580
A documentary on the siege
says that phone had no switch,

29
00:02:21,851 --> 00:02:24,328
so police could listen to whatever was said near it.

30
00:02:24,941 --> 00:02:27,580
Max Vernon was one of the police negotiators.

31
00:02:28,153 --> 00:02:29,829
Here he is on the pressure.

32
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:32,580
The main pressure is the fact

33
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,900
that if you make a mistake, somebody dies.

34
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:43,300
I mean, I've never, ever come across
a situation in my life before or since.

35
00:02:43,727 --> 00:02:47,564
On the first night the police began drilling
through the wall to place microphones.

36
00:02:48,095 --> 00:02:53,875
Harris says that when a nervous gunman heard
the noise, he and PC Lock told him it was mice.

37
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:56,242
Here is Harris telling it.

38
00:02:57,183 --> 00:03:00,733
And they said, what are mice? What are these mice?

39
00:03:01,583 --> 00:03:06,133
Oh, little animals, little animals,
rats, little animals like rats.

40
00:03:06,883 --> 00:03:10,533
So they looked a bit suspiciously,
and of course I joined in.

41
00:03:10,915 --> 00:03:14,261
On the second day, the gunmen
released Chris Cramer, who was ill.

42
00:03:14,854 --> 00:03:20,291
By the third day, the history records, Salim had
dropped the demand for the ninety one prisoners,

43
00:03:20,662 --> 00:03:23,376
and wanted only an aircraft to leave the country.

44
00:03:24,087 --> 00:03:25,630
The Home Office history says that

45
00:03:25,650 --> 00:03:30,208
on the third day Margaret Thatcher agreed
the SAS should decide how any assault was done,

46
00:03:30,459 --> 00:03:34,716
and said it was doubly important to succeed,
after the failed American rescue in Tehran.

47
00:03:35,167 --> 00:03:36,968
On Saturday night, 3 May,

48
00:03:36,988 --> 00:03:41,242
the gunmen said that unless police had
their statement broadcast by nine o'clock,

49
00:03:41,472 --> 00:03:42,983
two hostages would die.

50
00:03:43,654 --> 00:03:49,089
Police read it out at the scene, and the BBC
and the radio station LBC put it on air.

51
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:53,472
The gunmen heard it on Radio 2
and were jubilant, the history says.

52
00:03:54,243 --> 00:03:59,496
The negotiator told Salim he could hear it again
on Radio 4 at ten, and Salim thanked him.

53
00:04:00,127 --> 00:04:02,639
That night they released a Pakistani hostage,

54
00:04:02,890 --> 00:04:06,283
and the next evening the journalist
Mustapha Karkouti was let out.

55
00:04:06,714 --> 00:04:10,171
Karkouti says one of the gunmen
was always looking for a fight,

56
00:04:10,481 --> 00:04:12,856
and would walk into the room
with a grenade in his hand.

57
00:04:13,217 --> 00:04:15,967
He was looking for a fight all the time,

58
00:04:16,817 --> 00:04:18,967
and that was pretty dangerous.

59
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:23,654
All week the gunmen demanded that Arab
ambassadors come to guarantee their way out,

60
00:04:24,024 --> 00:04:26,176
and the government refused them a safe passage.

61
00:04:26,847 --> 00:04:32,325
On the fifth day, Tehran Radio broadcast a message
to the hostages that offered them up for martyrdom,

62
00:04:32,876 --> 00:04:35,009
and said Iran would not pay ransom.

63
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:46,962
By Monday 5 May, the sixth day, the Home Office
history says no further negotiation was possible.

64
00:04:47,494 --> 00:04:51,932
In the evening, Salim told the negotiators
the gunmen had killed one hostage,

65
00:04:52,182 --> 00:04:53,775
and would kill another in half an hour.

66
00:04:54,587 --> 00:04:57,204
Police recovered the body from outside the front door.

67
00:04:57,656 --> 00:05:00,734
He was Abbas Lavasani, an official of the embassy.

68
00:05:01,487 --> 00:05:04,263
The police commissioner rang
the government's crisis committee,

69
00:05:04,474 --> 00:05:06,708
and said he wished to send in the SAS.

70
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:10,595
The Home Secretary, William Whitelaw, said yes.

71
00:05:11,347 --> 00:05:15,943
At seven oh seven, control of the building
passed from the police to the Army.

72
00:05:16,373 --> 00:05:18,806
For the negotiators it was a bitter moment.

73
00:05:19,277 --> 00:05:20,889
Here is Max Vernon on it.

74
00:05:21,450 --> 00:05:26,900
I thought we'd failed. We'd failed to do the job,
to get the hostages out unharmed

75
00:05:27,150 --> 00:05:29,800
and the terrorists into custody. Which in fact is true.

76
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:33,132
Vernon's last job was to keep Salim on the telephone.

77
00:05:33,602 --> 00:05:36,484
A documentary says he talked
through plans for a coach

78
00:05:36,504 --> 00:05:39,416
to Heathrow while the SAS climbed down their ropes.

79
00:05:40,207 --> 00:05:45,563
Two SAS teams were in position on the roof and at
the back, and the operation had a name: Nimrod.

80
00:05:46,083 --> 00:05:52,133
Basically, it's like a plastic explosive, so it will cut
a hole through a wall, a window, a door, whatever.

81
00:05:52,729 --> 00:05:56,155
At twenty three minutes past
seven, the order came: go.

82
00:06:01,648 --> 00:06:07,655
ITN, the company that made the news for ITV, had
secretly set up a camera at the back of the embassy.

83
00:06:08,268 --> 00:06:14,069
When two men in black dropped onto a balcony,
ITN's director told his control room to put him on air.

84
00:06:14,841 --> 00:06:20,183
ITV came straight from the end of Coronation
Street, and its first sound on air was explosions.

85
00:06:20,754 --> 00:06:26,636
The BBC was showing a John Wayne film on one
channel, and the World Snooker final on the other.

86
00:06:27,328 --> 00:06:31,129
The snooker tournament carried the name
of its cigarette sponsor: Embassy.

87
00:06:31,761 --> 00:06:36,456
At seven thirty one, the BBC broke
in with live pictures on both channels.

88
00:06:37,187 --> 00:06:39,839
Kate Adie was reporting for the BBC outside.

89
00:06:40,330 --> 00:06:44,423
As the shooting began, she threw herself
under a car, and called for a microphone.

90
00:06:45,094 --> 00:06:46,987
A documentary says that at the back,

91
00:06:47,438 --> 00:06:51,315
one soldier's rope snagged as stun
grenades set the curtains alight.

92
00:06:51,846 --> 00:06:55,783
His colleagues cut him down,
and, burned, he carried on.

93
00:06:56,455 --> 00:07:00,056
Then a man appeared on the first floor
balcony, moving away from the flames.

94
00:07:00,617 --> 00:07:05,517
The first hostage to come out was
BBC sound recordist Sim Harris.

95
00:07:05,617 --> 00:07:09,167
He scrambled from the balcony
into the adjacent building.

96
00:07:09,547 --> 00:07:12,721
The National Army Museum puts
the raid at seventeen minutes,

97
00:07:12,871 --> 00:07:15,123
from seven twenty three to twenty to eight.

98
00:07:15,494 --> 00:07:20,330
Nineteen hostages came out alive,
but one hostage died in the assault.

99
00:07:20,841 --> 00:07:25,463
Five of the six gunmen were killed,
and the sixth, Fowzi Nejad, survived.

100
00:07:25,874 --> 00:07:33,030
The BBC's own minutes, two days later, confirmed
that ITN on ITV had been first with live pictures.

101
00:07:38,522 --> 00:07:42,308
The BBC's main bulletin that night
counted the gunmen like this.

102
00:07:43,190 --> 00:07:47,350
It ended with three gunmen dead,
one in hospital and another in police custody.

103
00:07:47,701 --> 00:07:50,126
The next day, Whitelaw told the House of Commons

104
00:07:50,146 --> 00:07:53,583
that four gunmen were believed dead
and another was in police custody.

105
00:07:54,074 --> 00:07:56,689
The final count was five dead and one survivor.

106
00:07:57,321 --> 00:07:58,493
In the Commons that day,

107
00:07:58,603 --> 00:08:01,167
the former Home Secretary Merlyn Rees spoke

108
00:08:01,187 --> 00:08:04,503
of the way the operation had been
seen on television the night before.

109
00:08:05,175 --> 00:08:11,142
Nejad was tried at the Old Bailey,
sentenced to life in 1981, and freed in 2008.

110
00:08:11,954 --> 00:08:15,667
Afterwards it was alleged that two gunmen
were shot while trying to surrender.

111
00:08:16,177 --> 00:08:20,110
A documentary says a coroner's
jury cleared all the soldiers involved.

112
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:25,053
The Home Office history says the resolution
was hailed as a success at home and abroad.

113
00:08:25,463 --> 00:08:31,277
But in the sense that five gunmen and two
hostages died, it adds, it was not a success.

114
00:08:31,948 --> 00:08:33,611
It says negotiation failed

115
00:08:33,631 --> 00:08:38,449
in the end because the government would not
give the gunmen a safe way out of the country.

116
00:08:39,121 --> 00:08:42,614
And it calls the achievement
of the military operation considerable,

117
00:08:43,004 --> 00:08:45,957
with six armed men holding more than fifty rooms.

118
00:08:46,588 --> 00:08:52,699
The journalist Ben Macintyre, in a recent book, says
Iraq's intelligence service organised the operation.

119
00:08:52,719 --> 00:08:57,096
Iraq invaded Iran that September.

120
00:08:57,528 --> 00:09:02,390
A documentary says the news cameras caught
only a fragment of what was happening inside.

121
00:09:03,641 --> 00:09:08,416
For six days the gunmen wanted the world
to listen, and in the end the world watched.

122
00:09:09,688 --> 00:09:14,194
When the cameras are already rolling,
should a rescue like that ever be shown live?

